# Runtime Memory APIs

> Read, write, and retrieve project memory from a Codebolt agent.

These examples run inside a connected agent or action block. Use IDs from the current project.

```js

```

## Memory Ingestion — `codebolt.memoryIngestion`

Create a manual pipeline once, or use the ID of an existing active pipeline with the same inputs. This example saves review decisions by ID in the KV store.

```js
const created = await codebolt.memoryIngestion.create({
  id: 'review-decisions', label: 'Review decisions', trigger: 'manual',
  processors: [{
    id: 'parse', type: 'parser', input: 'event.payload.record',
    output: 'record', params: { format: 'json' },
  }],
  routing: [{ from: 'record', write_to: { type: 'kv', instanceId: 'reviews', namespace: 'decisions' } }],
});
if (!created.success) throw new Error(created.error);
const pipelineId = created.data.pipeline.id;
const active = await codebolt.memoryIngestion.activate(pipelineId);
if (!active.success) throw new Error(active.error);
```

Send the same payload you would use in the [desktop ingestion example](../../02_using-codebolt/05d_memory/06_memory-ingestion.md).

```js
const write = await codebolt.memoryIngestion.execute({
  pipelineId, payload: { record: JSON.stringify({ id: 'review-1', decision: 'accepted' }) },
});
if (!write.success) throw new Error(write.error);
console.log(write.data.result);
```

Use `list()` to find pipelines under `data.pipelines`. See [Memory Ingestion](./02f_memory-ingestion.md) for triggers and destinations.

## Persistent Memory — `codebolt.persistentMemory`

Run a configured retrieval pipeline by its memory definition ID. Supply the variables that definition expects.

```js
const read = await codebolt.persistentMemory.executeRetrieval(memoryId, {
  query: 'Which review decisions were accepted?',
});
if (!read.success) throw new Error(read.error);
console.log(read.data.result);
```

Use `list()` to find definitions under `data.memories`. See [Persistent Memory](./02d_persistent-memory.md) for retrieval steps.

## Context Rule Engine — `codebolt.contextRuleEngine`

Test the rules for a planning request before running retrieval.

```js
const rules = await codebolt.contextRuleEngine.evaluate({
  scope_variables: {}, additional_variables: { phase: 'planning' },
});
if (!rules.success) throw new Error(rules.error);
console.log(rules.data);
```

## Context Assembly — `codebolt.contextAssembly`

Use the thread ID and a persistent memory definition ID from your project.

```js
const response = await codebolt.contextAssembly.getContext({
  scope_variables: { thread: { thread_id: threadId } },
  explicit_memory: [memoryId],
  constraints: { max_tokens: 800 },
});
if (!response.success) throw new Error(response.error);
const assembled = response.data.context;
console.log(assembled.context, assembled.trace, assembled.meta);
```

See [Context Assembly](./02g_context-assembly.md) for rule-based selection and [Context Compaction](./02h_context-compaction.md) for reducing conversation history.

## KV Store — `codebolt.kvStore`

Store a preference under a key in an existing instance.

```js
await codebolt.kvStore.set(instanceId, 'preferences', 'theme', 'dark');
const saved = await codebolt.kvStore.get(instanceId, 'preferences', 'theme');
if (saved.data?.exists) console.log(saved.data.value);
```

See [KV Store](./02e_kv-store.md#sdk-reference) to create an instance and query records.

## Vector Store — `codebolt.vectordb`

Use `codebolt.vectordb` for vector-item operations inside an agent.

```ts
const vector = await codebolt.vectordb.getVector('architecture-decision');
```

To manage a document collection, use the server’s `/vectorDB/collections` API. For example, to update a collection’s description, send `PUT /vectorDB/collections/:id` with `{"description":"Completed task summaries"}`. Delete a collection with `DELETE /vectorDB/collections/:id`. The [Vector DB examples](./02a_vector-db.md#sdk-reference) show how to create a collection, index and query documents, and add or remove chunks.

## Knowledge Graph — `codebolt.knowledgeGraph`

List records of a kind defined by your graph's instance template.

```js
const records = await codebolt.knowledgeGraph.listMemoryRecords(instanceId, { kind: 'Module' });
if (!records.success) throw new Error(records.error);
console.log(records.data);
```

See [Knowledge Graph](./02c_knowledge-graph.md#sdk-reference) to define a template, add records and edges, and query a view.

## Episodic Memory — `codebolt.episodicMemory`

Create a memory thread, append a decision, and read it back.

```js
const created = await codebolt.episodicMemory.createMemory({ title: 'Payment module decisions' });
if (!created.success) throw new Error(created.error);
const memoryId = created.data.id;
const appended = await codebolt.episodicMemory.appendEvent({
  memoryId, event_type: 'decision', emitting_agent_id: agentId,
  payload: { decision: 'Extract PaymentService' },
});
if (!appended.success) throw new Error(appended.error);
const recent = await codebolt.episodicMemory.queryEvents({ memoryId, event_type: 'decision', lastCount: 10 });
if (recent.success) console.log(recent.data.events);
```

Use `archiveMemory({ memoryId })` when you want to archive this thread.

## Markdown & JSON Memory — `codebolt.memory`

The application manages Markdown and JSON as memory threads. The example below uses the desktop server's thread endpoints. Set the server address to the one serving your project.

```js
const base = 'http://localhost:2719/memories';
async function writeMemory(path, method, body) {
  const response = await fetch(base + path, {
    method, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(body),
  });
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(await response.text());
  return (await response.json()).thread;
}

const noteId = crypto.randomUUID();
await writeMemory('/markdown/threads', 'POST', { threadId: noteId, title: 'Payment decisions' });
await writeMemory('/markdown/threads/' + noteId + '/content', 'PUT', {
  content: '## Decision\nExtract PaymentService.',
});

const recordId = crypto.randomUUID();
await writeMemory('/json/threads', 'POST', { threadId: recordId, title: 'Module status' });
await writeMemory('/json/threads/' + recordId + '/data', 'PUT', {
  data: { module: 'payments', status: 'reviewed' },
});
```

Reuse the thread ID to update its contents. To list thread types, including to-do and context threads, use `GET /memories/threads`.

# Context Compaction

> Preview compaction for a conversation thread and run it from an agent.

Use compaction when a conversation has grown long and you want to reduce the history carried into subsequent turns. To retrieve information from project stores, use [Context Assembly](./02g_context-assembly.md).

## Preview a thread

From a connected agent, pass an existing **context thread ID**. The server uses the project’s saved default profile unless you pass a `profileId`. A dry run executes the workflow, including any model-backed summarization steps, and returns the projected messages without replacing the stored context.

```js
const preview = await codebolt.contextCompaction.dryRun({ threadId });
if (!preview.success) throw new Error(preview.error);
const result = preview.data.result;
if (!result.success) throw new Error(result.error);
if (result.deferred) {
  console.log(result.deferredReason);
} else {
  console.log(result.stats.estimatedBeforeTokens, result.stats.estimatedAfterTokens);
  console.log(result.stepResults, result.preview?.messages);
}
```

To apply compaction to that thread, call `codebolt.contextCompaction.run({ threadId })`. A deferred dry run does not schedule another preview. Retry after the active model or tool round finishes. An applied run can be queued and retried by the server.

Use `getConfig()` to read the configuration and `getStepSpecs()` to inspect the available pipeline steps.

See the [desktop compaction panel](../../02_using-codebolt/02_surfaces/02_desktop-app/03c_panels/04e_memory-features/05_context-compaction.md) for the settings and the [SDK reference](../../05_reference/02_codeboltjs/10_api-access/contextCompaction/index.md) for method arguments.

# Hooks and Action Blocks

> Hooks and action blocks are the event-driven layer of Codebolt's memory system. Hooks watch for lifecycle events and fire automatically

Use a session hook to run a custom action block when an agent finishes. This example reads recent events from an episodic memory you choose and saves a Markdown summary.

## Action blocks

[Create an action block](../03_agent-extensions/05_action-blocks/03_authoring.md) named `memory-summarizer`. Put this handler in its entry point and build the block. It needs a configured inference provider and an episodic memory containing events.

**`index.js`:**

```javascript

codebolt.onActionBlockInvocation(async ({ params }) => {
  const { memoryId, noteId, serverUrl } = params;
  const response = await codebolt.episodicMemory.queryEvents({ memoryId, lastMinutes: 60 });
  if (!response.success || !response.data) throw new Error('Could not read episodic events');
  const { completion } = await codebolt.llm.inference({
    input: [{ type: 'message', role: 'user',
      content: 'Summarize these events as Markdown: ' + JSON.stringify(response.data.events) }],
  });
  if (!completion.output_text) throw new Error('The model returned no summary');
  const saved = await fetch(serverUrl + '/memories/markdown/threads/' + encodeURIComponent(noteId) + '/content', {
    method: 'PUT', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ content: completion.output_text }),
  });
  if (!saved.ok) throw new Error(await saved.text());
  return { summary: completion.output_text };
});
```

### Calling an action block from an agent

Use the registered block ID, an episodic memory ID, your server URL, and the ID of an existing [Markdown thread](./02_runtime-memory-apis.md). Use a dedicated note because each run replaces its contents.

```typescript
const response = await codebolt.actionBlock.start(actionBlockId, { memoryId, noteId, serverUrl }, {
  waitForCompletion: true,
});
if (!response.success) throw new Error(response.error || 'Summarization failed');
console.log(response.result);
```

## Hooks

Open **Agents → Hooks** or create a `.hook` file under `.codebolt/hooks/`. Set the IDs and server address to the same values you used when testing the block.

```json
{
  "name": "Save session summary",
  "enabled": true,
  "event": "agentSessionEnd",
  "handler": {
    "type": "actionBlock",
    "actionBlockId": "YOUR_ACTION_BLOCK_ID",
    "inputs": {
      "memoryId": "YOUR_EPISODIC_MEMORY_ID",
      "noteId": "YOUR_MARKDOWN_THREAD_ID",
      "serverUrl": "http://localhost:2719"
    }
  }
}
```

The hook passes the lifecycle event to the block as `params.hookInput` and the configured memory ID as `params.memoryId`. With this configuration, each invocation reads the same episodic memory. To summarize different memories by session, have your agent pass the corresponding ID when it calls the block directly.

Inspect the invocation in **Agents → Hooks** and check the saved Markdown note. See [Hooks](../../02_using-codebolt/08d_auto-interactivity/04_hooks.md) for supported events and matchers.

# Memory Evaluation

> Test whether an agent retrieves and uses a known project record.

Evaluating memory means measuring whether agents are retrieving the right information at the right time and whether the pipeline that writes to storage is producing quality embeddings and knowledge graph entries. Use a known record and an expected answer to measure recall.

## The eval model

The eval system has four composable primitives:

| Primitive | What it represents |
|---|---|
| **Subject** | The thing being evaluated — an agent, an action block |
| **Task** | A test case: input instructions + one or more evaluators |
| **Suite** | A collection of subjects × tasks |
| **Run** | An execution of a suite; produces scored results |

Memory-focused evals treat **retrieval quality** and **context relevance** as the metrics. You design tasks that probe specific retrieval scenarios, run them against the subject agent, and score the output.

---

The examples below call the `/evals` HTTP API on a running Codebolt server. Define this helper once. It returns the JSON envelope from the controller.

```js
async function evalRequest(path, method = 'GET', body) {
  const response = await fetch('http://localhost:2719/evals' + path, {
    method, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: body === undefined ? undefined : JSON.stringify(body),
  });
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(await response.text());
  return response.json();
}
```

## Evaluator types

### `expected-output`

Compare the agent's response against a reference answer. Match modes:

| Mode | Matches when… |
|---|---|
| `exact` | Response equals expected exactly |
| `contains` | Response includes the expected substring |
| `regex` | Response matches a regular expression |
| `semantic` | Response contains the expected text, ignoring case |

```typescript
const { task } = await evalRequest('/tasks', 'POST', {
  name: 'What database do we use for the knowledge graph?',
  subjectType: 'agent',
  instruction: { type: 'text', textPrompt: 'What database do we use for the knowledge graph?' },
  environment: { type: 'local' },
  evaluators: [{
    id: 'ev-kg-answer',
    name: 'KG answer check',
    type: 'expected-output',
    weight: 1,
    expectedOutput: 'Kuzu',
    matchMode: 'contains'
  }]
});
```

Use `semantic` mode when capitalization may differ:

```typescript
{
  type: 'expected-output',
  expectedOutput: 'kuzu',
  matchMode: 'semantic'
}
```

### `agent-judge`

A separate judge agent evaluates the output against a rubric. Best for nuanced memory quality questions.

```typescript
{
  type: 'agent-judge',
  judgeAgentId: 'quality-judge',
  judgePrompt: `Score 0-100: Did the agent's answer correctly use recalled memory rather than hallucinating? 
  Agent answer: {{output}}
  Provide a score and one-sentence reasoning.`
}
```

### `script`

The evaluator exposes the subject’s response in the `EVAL_OUTPUT` environment variable. Print a JSON object with a score from 0 to 100 and a reason.

```js
const scriptEvaluator = {
  id: 'mentions-db', name: 'Mentions database', type: 'script', weight: 1,
  script: `node -e 'const match = process.env.EVAL_OUTPUT.includes("Kuzu"); console.log(JSON.stringify({ score: match ? 100 : 0, reasoning: "Database name check" }))'`,
};
```

### `deliberation`

The evaluator opens a deliberation with the listed agents and immediately requests the winning option. If no winner is available yet, it uses a score of 50.

```typescript
{
  type: 'deliberation',
  deliberationAgentIds: ['judge-a', 'judge-b', 'judge-c'],
  deliberationRounds: 2
}
```

---

## Designing memory eval tasks

The key is to write tasks that exercise specific retrieval paths, then check whether the agent's answer shows evidence of using recalled memory (not just LLM knowledge).

### Test vector recall

```typescript
await evalRequest('/tasks', 'POST', {
  name: 'Summarise the decisions we made about the payment module in the last sprint.',
  subjectType: 'agent',
  instruction: { type: 'text', textPrompt: 'Summarise the decisions we made about the payment module in the last sprint.' },
  environment: { type: 'local' },
  evaluators: [{
    id: 'recall-check', name: 'Recall check', weight: 1,
    type: 'agent-judge',
    judgeAgentId: 'memory-judge',
    judgePrompt: 'Did the answer reference specific decisions from memory? Score 0-100.'
  }]
});
```

### Test knowledge graph recall

```typescript
await evalRequest('/tasks', 'POST', {
  name: 'Which functions call processPayment?',
  subjectType: 'agent',
  instruction: { type: 'text', textPrompt: 'Which functions call processPayment?' },
  environment: { type: 'local' },
  evaluators: [{
    id: 'recall-check', name: 'Recall check', weight: 1,
    type: 'expected-output',
    expectedOutput: 'chargeStripe',
    matchMode: 'contains'
  }]
});
```

### Test context rule injection

```typescript
await evalRequest('/tasks', 'POST', {
  name: 'Create a PR for the auth changes following our coding standards.',
  subjectType: 'agent',
  instruction: { type: 'text', textPrompt: 'Create a PR for the auth changes following our coding standards.' },
  environment: { type: 'local' },
  evaluators: [{
    id: 'recall-check', name: 'Recall check', weight: 1,
    type: 'agent-judge',
    judgeAgentId: 'style-judge',
    judgePrompt: 'Did the PR description follow the style guide? Score 0-100.'
  }]
});
```

---

## Running an eval suite

```typescript
// Use the task ID returned by POST /evals/tasks and an installed agent’s ID.
const { suite } = await evalRequest('/suites', 'POST', {
  name: 'Memory recall', taskIds: [task.id],
});
const { run } = await evalRequest('/runs', 'POST', {
  suiteId: suite.id,
  subjects: [{ type: 'agent', referenceId: agentId, name: 'Memory agent' }],
});
await evalRequest(`/runs/${run.id}/start`, 'POST');

// Run this again to check progress. Results belong to the run.
const { run: latest } = await evalRequest(`/runs/${run.id}`);
console.log(latest.status, latest.results);
```

## Optimization loop

Run the same suite against the same agent and stored records before and after changing one retrieval setting. Save the two run IDs and compare their task scores and answers. Use the context assembly trace to check whether the expected record reached the agent.

## Evaluating action blocks directly

The eval system also supports `action-block` as a subject type:

```typescript
const { subject } = await evalRequest('/subjects', 'POST', {
  type: 'action-block', referenceId: 'memory-summarizer', name: 'Memory summarizer',
});
const { task: blockTask } = await evalRequest('/tasks', 'POST', {
  name: 'Summarize a known memory', subjectType: 'action-block',
  instruction: { type: 'text', textPrompt: JSON.stringify({ memoryId }) },
  environment: { type: 'local' },
  evaluators: [{ id: 'topic', name: 'Mentions the topic', type: 'expected-output',
    weight: 1, expectedOutput: 'database', matchMode: 'contains' }],
});
const { run: blockRun } = await evalRequest('/runs', 'POST', {
  taskId: blockTask.id, subjects: [subject],
});
await evalRequest(`/runs/${blockRun.id}/start`, 'POST');
```

## Viewing results in the UI

Open the **Eval** panel and select the run to inspect its results. An evaluator scores the subject's output. To investigate a low score, compare that output with the memory records and the [assembly trace](./02g_context-assembly.md).

