- [Hooks and action blocks](./03_hooks-and-action-blocks.md) — action blocks as eval subjects

# Optimization Patterns

> Practical recipes for keeping memory lean, relevant, and fast. These patterns combine the hook system, action blocks, ingestion pipelines, and direct SDK calls.

Use these checks when a memory query returns stale, duplicate, or unexpected information.

## Save a session summary

Follow the [session hook example](./03_hooks-and-action-blocks.md) to read recent episodic events and save a Markdown note. If you need to reduce the conversation history sent on subsequent turns, preview [context compaction](./02h_context-compaction.md).

## Review stale vector documents

List the documents in the affected collection. Use the source URI to identify the document you want to refresh. Upsert its current text using the same URI, or delete the document if you have removed the source. See the [Vector DB examples](./02a_vector-db.md#sdk-reference) for both operations.

## Find possible duplicate graph records

Pass a graph instance ID to this custom action block. It returns groups of similarly named functions for review. Functions in different files can legitimately share a name.

```javascript

codebolt.onActionBlockInvocation(async ({ params: { instanceId } }) => {
  // Find all Function nodes
  const response = await codebolt.knowledgeGraph.listMemoryRecords(instanceId, { kind: 'Function' });

  if (!response.success || !response.data) throw new Error("Could not list records");
  const records = response.data;

  // Group by normalized name
  const groups = Object.create(null);
  for (const record of records) {
    const key = record.attributes.name?.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '');
    if (!key) continue;
    if (!groups[key]) groups[key] = [];
    groups[key].push(record);
  }

  return { candidates: Object.values(groups).filter(group => group.length > 1) };
});
```

## Compare context budgets

Run the same request twice with the same memory definition and thread. Inspect which items fit in each budget.

```js
for (const max_tokens of [400, 800]) {
  const response = await codebolt.contextAssembly.getContext({
    scope_variables: { thread: { thread_id: threadId } },
    explicit_memory: [memoryId], constraints: { max_tokens },
  });
  if (!response.success) throw new Error(response.error);
  const assembled = response.data.context;
  console.log(max_tokens, assembled.context, assembled.trace, assembled.meta);
}
```

## Invalidate a cached value

When your agent updates a source, clear the corresponding cache key after the update succeeds. This example assumes your agent stores the parsed schema under `schema-cache/current`.

```js
const cleared = await codebolt.kvStore.delete(instanceId, 'schema-cache', 'current');
if (!cleared.success) throw new Error(cleared.error);
console.log(cleared.data.deleted);
```

## Test a retrieval change

Use a known fact in project memory to create a [recall evaluation](./05_memory-evaluation.md). Run the same task before and after editing your retrieval definition. Compare both the answer and the assembly trace to see whether the expected record was retrieved.

# Discord

> The discord-channel plugin connects a Discord bot to Codebolt. It uses discord

# Discord Integration

The `discord-channel` plugin connects a Discord bot to Codebolt. It uses [`discord.js`](https://discord.js.org) v14 and supports text messages, file attachments, `@mention` routing in guild channels, and full DM support.

## Prerequisites

1. Create a Discord application at [discord.com/developers](https://discord.com/developers/applications)
2. Add a Bot to the application and copy the **Bot Token**
3. Enable the **Message Content Intent** in the Bot settings
4. Invite the bot to your server with `bot` + `applications.commands` scopes

## Installation

Install the plugin from the Codebolt registry:

Open **Marketplace → Plugins** and select the Discord channel plugin if it is available for your workspace.

Or add it to your project's `.codebolt/plugins.yaml`:

```yaml
plugins:
  - id: discord-channel
```

## Configuration

| Field | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `botToken` | Yes | Bot token from the Discord Developer Portal |
| `agentId` | No | Default agent to route messages to. Uses project default if omitted. |
| `threadStrategy` | No | `per-user` (default) · `per-conversation` · `per-message` · `single` |

Configure via the Codebolt UI (Settings → Plugins → discord-channel → Configure) or in `.codebolt/channels.json`.

## Message routing

**Guild channels** (servers): The bot only responds to messages that `@mention` it. Other messages are ignored.

**DMs**: All messages are forwarded to the agent.

```
User in #general: @MyBot what's the status?
  → plugin.gateway.routeMessage({ threadStrategy: 'per-user', userId: user.id, … })

User in DM: what's the status?
  → same path, no mention required
```

## Thread strategies

| Strategy | Recommended for |
|---|---|
| `per-user` | Personal assistants — one conversation per Discord user |
| `per-conversation` | Server bots — one thread per Discord channel |
| `per-message` | Stateless Q&A — no memory between messages |

## Attachment handling

The plugin maps Discord attachments to Codebolt's normalized format:

```ts
// Discord attachment → GatewayMessage metadata
{
  metadata: {
    attachments: [
      { type: 'image', url: attachment.url, name: attachment.name },
      { type: 'file',  url: attachment.url, name: attachment.name },
    ]
  }
}
```

Agent replies with attachments are sent back using `channel.send()` with a file buffer.

## Message chunking

Discord has a 2,000-character limit per message. The plugin automatically splits long agent replies into multiple messages.

## Minimal custom implementation

If you need custom behavior beyond the first-party plugin, here is the core pattern:

```ts

  const client = new Client({
    intents: [
      GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
      GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
      GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
      GatewayIntentBits.DirectMessages,
    ],
  });

  // Register the channel with the gateway
  const registration = await plugin.gateway.registerChannel({
    name: 'Discord',
    platform: 'discord',
    agentId: config.agentId,
    threadStrategy: config.threadStrategy ?? 'per-user',
  });
  const channelId = registration.data?.channelId;
  if (!registration.success || !channelId) throw new Error(registration.error || 'Channel registration failed');

  // Forward incoming messages
  client.on('messageCreate', async (message) => {
    if (message.author.bot) return;

    // Guild channels require @mention
    if (message.guild && !message.mentions.has(client.user!)) return;

    await plugin.gateway.routeMessage({
      source: 'channel',
      sourceId: plugin.pluginId,
      threadStrategy: config.threadStrategy ?? 'per-user',
      agentId: config.agentId,
      userId: message.author.id,
      externalThreadId: message.channelId,
      text: message.content.replace(/<@!?\d+>/g, '').trim(),
      replyTo: {
        channelId,
        externalThreadId: message.channelId,
        userId: message.author.id,
      },
    });
  });

  // Send agent replies back to Discord
  plugin.gateway.onReply(async (reply) => {
    if (!reply.externalThreadId) return;
    const channel = await client.channels.fetch(reply.externalThreadId);
    if (channel?.isTextBased()) {
      // Chunk at 2000 chars
      const chunks = reply.text.match(/.{1,2000}/gs) ?? [reply.text];
      for (const chunk of chunks) {
        await channel.send(chunk);
      }
    }
  });

  await client.login(config.botToken);
}
```

